  3	// Where an exception was thrown, so a report's stack is the throw's and not
  4	// the handler's that reported it (AGENTS.md, Error handling: a failure
  5	// travels up unchanged to one handler). Boost 1.84 in this build cannot do
  6	// this (boost::stacktrace::from_current_exception came in 1.85), nor can
  7	// Sentry's SDK, so the app records each C++ throw itself.
  8	//
  9	// macOS and Linux: every throw from the app's own code and its static
 10	// libraries (wx, boost, OCCT...) passes the app's __cxa_throw. A throw inside
 11	// the system's C++ library (libc++.dylib on macOS) is not seen. Linux's
 12	// libstdc++ calls the app's, so it is. A throw is found by its thrown
 13	// object's address, and the C++ runtime gives a freed exception's address to
 14	// the next one, so a record holds its exception from the app's first catch
 15	// of it (ThrowStack.cpp, hold_caught): a throw that was not seen gets no
 16	// stack, not an earlier throw's.
 17	// Windows: a vectored exception handler sees every C++ throw in the process
 18	// and records it on the throwing thread; a report finds the exception it
 19	// handles there by its thrown object.
